mcp/connect.markdown:
Pick where your Stirling PDF runs, then add its endpoint to your client.




Endpoint: `https://api.stirling.com/mcp`

Open **Settings → Developer → MCP Server**. It shows **Your MCP endpoint** and, under **Connect your AI assistant**, a snippet for each client. Paste it into the file shown, then restart the client. On first use you sign in with your Stirling account and click **Allow access** on the **Authorize access** page. There is no key to copy.

Claude Code (`.mcp.json`). For VS Code, use the same in `.vscode/mcp.json` with `servers` in place of `mcpServers`:

```json
{"mcpServers": {"stirling-pdf": {"type": "http", "url": "https://api.stirling.com/mcp"}}}
```

Codex CLI (`~/.codex/config.toml`):

```toml
[mcp_servers.stirling-pdf]
url = "https://api.stirling.com/mcp"
```

Claude Desktop (`claude_desktop_config.json`) reaches remote servers through the `mcp-remote` bridge, which needs Node.js:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "stirling-pdf": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "mcp-remote", "https://api.stirling.com/mcp"]
    }
  }
}
```

Guests can't connect MCP clients. Every tool call counts towards your team's usage, like API calls ([Plans, credits and billing](doc:cloud/plans-and-billing)).




Endpoint: `https://your-server/mcp`, on the same host and port as Stirling PDF.

MCP is off by default. An admin turns it on and picks how clients sign in ([MCP server settings](doc:server/automation/mcp-server)). OAuth is the default, so ask your admin which mode is set before you use an API key.

- **API key mode:** create a key under **Settings → Preferences → API Keys** and send it as `X-API-KEY: <key>` or `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.
- **OAuth mode:** add the endpoint with no key. The client finds your identity provider at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` and signs you in.

Claude Code in API key mode (`.mcp.json`):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "stirling-pdf": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://your-server/mcp",
      "headers": { "X-API-KEY": "your-stirling-api-key" }
    }
  }
}
```

Claude Desktop through `mcp-remote` (needs Node.js). In OAuth mode, leave out the `--header` line and `mcp-remote` runs the sign-in:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "stirling-pdf": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": [
        "-y", "mcp-remote", "https://your-server/mcp",
        "--header", "X-API-KEY:your-stirling-api-key"
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

To test the connection, run `npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector`, choose the streamable HTTP transport, enter your `/mcp` URL and add the same header.

PDF tool calls count as automation, so they use the server's allowance even with a Team licence ([Processing allowance](doc:server/licensing/connect-to-stirling-cloud)).

server/production.markdown:
## 1. Install a login build and pin the version

- Use the `latest` or `latest-fat` image, the Helm chart, or `Stirling-PDF-with-login.jar`. See [Install with Docker](doc:server/install/docker), [Install on Kubernetes](doc:server/install/kubernetes) or [Install with Java (JAR)](doc:server/install/jar).
- Pin a version tag instead of `latest` so upgrades happen when you choose.
- Mount at least `/configs` and `/storage`, and `/customFiles` if you use branding or signature files.
- Size it from [Performance](doc:server/operations/performance): at least 4 GB of memory (about 6 GB for `latest-fat`), more for larger teams or with the AI engine on.

## 2. Secure the admin account

- Replace the default `admin` password at first sign-in, or create your own first admin before the first start. See [First login](doc:server/server).
- Keep `SECURITY_ENABLELOGIN` at its default of `true`.

## 3. Serve it over HTTPS

**Behind a reverse proxy or load balancer** (most setups):

- Forward requests to port `8080` with the `X-Forwarded-Proto`, `X-Forwarded-Host` and `X-Forwarded-Port` headers.
- Accept uploads as large as `system.fileUploadLimit` (2000 MB by default).
- Turn off response buffering and allow 30-minute read timeouts for long AI and Processor runs.
- To serve under a sub-path such as `/pdf`, set `SYSTEM_ROOTURIPATH=/pdf` and forward the prefix unchanged.

**HTTPS in Stirling PDF itself:** put your keystore in `configs/` and set `server.ssl` in `custom_settings.yml`. See [Extra settings](doc:server/customisation/extra-settings).

Then set the public addresses the server hands out in links. `backendUrl` is required for SAML single sign-on, and `frontendUrl` is used in invite emails, share links, mobile QR codes and the redirect after SAML sign-in:



    ```yaml
    system:
      backendUrl: https://pdf.example.com
      frontendUrl: https://pdf.example.com
    ```


    ```bash
    SYSTEM_BACKENDURL=https://pdf.example.com
    SYSTEM_FRONTENDURL=https://pdf.example.com
    ```



### Example: nginx

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name pdf.example.com;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name pdf.example.com;

    ssl_certificate     /etc/ssl/certs/pdf.example.com.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/pdf.example.com.key;

    client_max_body_size 2000M;    # nginx allows 1 MB by default

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Port $server_port;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_buffering off;
        proxy_read_timeout 1800s;
        proxy_send_timeout 1800s;
    }
}
```

## 4. Set up sign-in and users

- Add people in **Settings → Workspace → Users → Invite people**. **Invite by email** needs [email configuration](doc:server/security/sign-in-and-security); otherwise use **Create account directly**.
- Connect your identity provider: [OAuth2 / OIDC single sign-on](doc:server/security/oauth-sso) works on every plan, [SAML single sign-on](doc:server/security/saml-sso) needs Enterprise.
- Failed sign-ins lock an account after 5 attempts for 120 minutes (`security.loginAttemptCount`, `security.loginResetTimeMinutes`). For IP bans, add [Fail2Ban](doc:server/security/fail2ban).

## 5. Decide what users can do

- Hide tools you don't want offered: see [Turn features on or off](doc:server/customisation/features-and-endpoints).
- Add your own legal links and login agreement in **Settings → Server → Legal & privacy**.
- Choose whether anonymous usage data is sent: see [Analytics and telemetry](doc:server/analytics-and-telemetry).

## 6. Back up

- Back up the whole `configs/` folder. It holds your settings, generated keys and the built-in database.
- The built-in database also writes a nightly backup to `configs/backup/db/`. Copy those off the server too.
- Back up `storage/`. It holds the files users save on the server.
- Back up `customFiles/` if you use it.
- On Team or Enterprise you can use PostgreSQL instead: see [Database](doc:server/storage/database).

## 7. Monitor

- Point uptime and load balancer health checks at `/api/v1/info/status` (after your root path, if you set one). It needs no sign-in.
- Logs are in `logs/`, or `docker logs stirling-pdf`.
- Team and Enterprise add Prometheus metrics: see [Usage monitoring](doc:server/operations/usage-monitoring). Enterprise adds **Usage Analytics** and **Audit log** under **Settings → Monitoring**.
- When something breaks, [Diagnostics](doc:server/operations/diagnostics) collects logs and settings into one archive.

## 8. Plan for load and updates

- Tune how many OCR and Office jobs run at once in [Process limits](doc:server/operations/process-limits) and [LibreOffice parallel processing](doc:server/operations/libreoffice). For several nodes, see [Clustering](doc:server/operations/clustering) (Team and Enterprise).
- Back up `configs/` before each update. Coming from V1? Read [Upgrading from V1](doc:server/upgrading/upgrading) first.
